A REVISION CAN ARRIVE UNDER A NEW NAME. dppo2025_v2.xsd sits beside an
UNCHANGED dppo2025.xsd and differs by one element: a repeatable
`dalsiaTransakcia` (further controlled transactions). A digest check on the
pinned name would report "no change" forever while the revision sat one URL
away. Both are kept here — v2 is what the 2025 version record files against,
because a superset accepts every document the original did — and the refresh
procedure now probes for the suffix.

The 2018 and 2019 schemas are NOT interchangeable despite defining the same
row set: 2019 adds the `optInt2` type. Nothing in a finished document says
which vzor it was written for, which is why the validity windows are recorded
here and enforced in the version records rather than inferred from a file.

REFRESH PROCEDURE
  1. Fetch each name above and compare the md5 with the line here.
  2. Check the STATUS CODE, not the size: a miss returns 404 with a
     ~1 245-byte HTML error page, a perfectly plausible file size.
  3. Probe the NEXT year AND a `_v2` suffix on every name already pinned.
     dppo2025_v2 is why; a year alone is not enough to find a revision.
